      "hypothesis_title": "Thalamic Spindle Modulation via Thalamocortical Coupling and White Matter Integrity in Sleep Phenotypes",
      "scientific_question": "Test whether Thalamic Spindle Modulation via Thalamocortical Coupling and White Matter Integrity in Sleep Phenotypes is supported by available analysis-ready variables.",
      "hypothesis_summary": "This hypothesis integrates thalamocortical coupling and white matter integrity to explain spindle modulation and sleep phenotypes, leveraging mechanisms from both P1 and P2.",
      "hypothesis_content": "The hypothesis proposes that thalamocortical coupling, modulated by both the refractory period of thalamic spindles and white matter fractional anisotropy (FA), influences spindle density and topographical patterns, thereby shaping sleep phenotypes. Epileptic thalamic spikes increase the refractory period, while FA in white matter tracts modulates the topographical distribution of spindle subtypes. Together, these factors interact to influence spindle density and sleep architecture.",
